Where AI can add value

AI-assisted review may help identify related concerns written in different words, prepare summaries for human review, or surface recurring themes across large volumes of records.

For example, “barrier moved,” “pedestrian route blocked” and “walking through vehicle area” may describe a related operational problem. They may also describe different situations. A reviewer needs the underlying records to decide.

A useful AI output should make its supporting evidence accessible and allow corrections. Review missed patterns as well as incorrect suggestions.

Do not treat AI-generated sentiment as a direct measure of trust, or use a software-generated culture score to rank individual workers. Language, reporting habits and missing context can distort the interpretation.

Compare fairly across locations

A container terminal, a bulk-handling facility and a passenger operation do not have identical work patterns.

Even within one organisation, reporting practices can differ by shift, contractor, language or supervisor. Comparisons should account for those differences.

Begin with each location’s own baseline. Use consistent definitions and document changes in workload, workforce composition and assessment methods.

Where results differ, investigate the context before assigning a ranking. The purpose is to identify useful improvements and transferable lessons.

A practical 90-day starting plan

Period	Focus	Expected output
Days 1–15	Define scope with operations, HSE and workforce representatives	Agreed questions, confidentiality arrangements and measures
Days 16–30	Establish a baseline through records, conversations and field observations	A documented starting picture with known limitations
Days 31–60	Address a small number of priority concerns	Assigned actions, visible feedback and implementation evidence
Days 61–90	Check effectiveness and report back to the workforce	Findings on what worked, what did not and what needs further attention
Ninety days can test whether the assessment and response process works. It should not be presented as proof that an organisation’s culture has permanently changed.

Review progress over a longer period and during different operating conditions. OSHA’s programme-evaluation guidance supports monitoring implementation, involving workers and adjusting the programme when weaknesses are identified. Read the evaluation guidance. Occupational Safety and Health Administration

What meaningful change looks like

A worker can explain what happened after raising a concern. A supervisor can describe how an operational conflict was resolved. An incoming shift understands what remains outstanding. A manager can show that a recurring problem was investigated and that the resulting change held up during real work.

Those are useful signs to look for.

